ion x y  by e reps which have low dimensions okay  you can truncate  the fourier expansion of x and y  by keeping just  the reps which have low dimension  and then we show two ways in which this  lemma can be used  one way is the so-called kernel method  we show that if you have an element in  the group which is in the kernel of  every rep  of  small dimension  then you can mix simply by  multiplying an element of the group by h  so f of g is just the deterministic  function which multiplies by h  and again the idea is that because h is  in the kernel  of every low dimension a rep and since  you can only you can approximate x y  with the reps of load dimension  uh multiplying by h does not change x y  this method works for a find in the  lamplighter group and  this can be verified that just by  looking  uh the reps of these of these groups but  it does not work for product groups  for product groups we do something  different  still relying on the  dilemma  the basic observation is that the e-reps  in product groups h to the n  are tensors  of the e-reps of h  and in particular the dimensions  multiply  so because by the lemma we can  approximate x y  by e reps with low dimensions  we must have that  we can approximate x y  with reps that have dimension  either one  or that that depend on few coordinates  right because if you had many  reps  of the basic group h which have  dimension larger than one  and you tensorize  because the dimensions multiply  you will get a  an rep for h to dn which has very large  dimensions but the lemma allows us to  ignore them  okay so we can approximate x y with the  reps which depend on few coordinates or  possibly have dimension one  now remember that the mixing was  by picking a uniform coordinate i  and then setting that coordinate to a  uniform conjugate  and the basic idea why this works is  that  when you pick a random i  the approximation of x y is unlikely to  depend on i  so whether or not you pick this  conjugate does not change the  distribution  and this concludes  our talk  thank you

